Playing Forward 
From rebuilding his life to coaching the next generation – a journey of connection, 
confidence and leadership 
 
Mustafa Mohmand “Zac” 
Mustafa  Mohmand  had  spent  almost  his  whole  life  around  football.  By  the  time  he 
reached Australia, it became a distant memory. 
"I totally forgot football," he says. He is not being casual. For months after arriving, he 
did not think about the game at all. 
 
MOSAIC MULTICULTURAL CONNECTIONS 
Mustafa’s Story 

 
Mosaic Multicultural Connections | Multicultural NSW 
Mustafa’s Story | Newcastle, NSW | 08 July 2026 
mosaicmc.org.au 
 
 
He grew up in a small garrison town, five or six big buildings, home to the families of 
men who worked for the military. There was not much there. But there was football 
every afternoon, on stone-covered pitches, and as a boy he watched it the way other 
children  watch  television.  He  was  seven  when  he  first  walked  onto  one  of  those 
grounds. "Slowly, slowly," he says, "it came closer to me." 
 
Mustafa aged 10, after a game of football with his friends, in Kandhar Daman, Afghanistan 
He  turned  out  to  be  good.  He  played  for  Kandahar's  provincial  side,  then  for 
Afghanistan's national youth teams, up through the age groups from the under-13s. 
Along the way he came to love one strange truth about the game: that every match 
happens  only  once.  "If  that  game  is  finished, it never  comes back,"  he  says.  "That's 
gone." So, he took each one as it came. 
Then the game, and everything around it, stopped having a future. 
His  family  had  supported  Australian  Defence  Force  efforts  back  home.  When  the 
Taliban returned in 2021, staying was no longer safe, and they left. "Home is a home. 
Home sweet home," Mustafa says. "But sometimes you have to make another decision, 
to leave your home, your relatives, everyone." They arrived in a new country with a bag 
of clothes and a heart filled with hope. 

 
Mosaic Multicultural Connections | Multicultural NSW 
Mustafa’s Story | Newcastle, NSW | 08 July 2026 
mosaicmc.org.au 
 
 
He did not arrive looking for a club. He did not watch matches. He did not think about 
the game at all. There was a family to hold together and a life to build from nothing, 
and  somewhere  in  that,  the  part  of  him  that  played  simply disappeared  into  the 
background. 
And what brought it back was not a football club. Mustafa first heard about Mosaic 
through his older brother, Sami, who had already settled in Australia and was working 
with the COMPACT program. Like most newly arrived families, he too leaned first on 
the people he already trusted. Through COMPACT, a program funded by Multicultural 
NSW for young people who arrive the way he had, Mustafa found his way back. 
 
Mustafa on a COMPACT trip to Canberra, ACT 
COMPACT  did  not  hand  him  a  ball.  It  took  him  on  trips,  to  Canberra,  to  Armidale, 
around  the  Hunter,  alongside  other  young  Australians  and  Aboriginal  communities. 
On paper, excursions. In practice, the thing that had to come first. He started talking 
to people  and understanding  them.  "I  got  really  better  in  communication,"  he  says. 
Only once that came back was there room for anything else. 

 
