He asked Maitland FC for a coaching trial, and they gave him the under-13s girls. He 
was good with them. Within two months the club moved him up to its senior women's 
side in the National Premier League, as the senior assistant coach and reserve-grade 
coach, where he still is. 
 
Mustafa with the Maitland FC team celebrating, Vs Newcastle Olympic FC, Cooks Square Park, 31/08/2025 
Last season that team went into a decider against Newcastle Olympic, the premiership 
on  the  line.  They  fell  a  goal  behind  before  half  time.  They  came  back  level,  kept 
pushing, and scored again in the final minutes to win it. Ask Mustafa about that night 
and he will not talk about himself. "They fought for it to the last whistle," he says, "and 
they got it." The credit, he insists, belongs to the players, the parents, the people in the 
stands. He keeps handing it away. 
On 3 June 2025, in the New South Wales Parliament, the Member for Maitland, Jenny 
Aitchison, read a Community Recognition Notice into the record. She thanked Mustafa 
for his commitment to football in the Maitland community, and quoted his club coach, 
Joe  Wright,  who  said  the  club  was  lucky  to  have  not  only  someone  of Mustafa's 
coaching calibre, but his character. Mustafa was not in the room. He learned of it by 
letter. Asked whether he had ever had anything like it back home, he answers twice. 
"Never. Never." It was never about the honour. It was about being seen. 

 
