Mustafa with Joseph Wright Head Coach of Maitland FC NPL Women’s NNSW, 22/11/2025 
Everything  he  does,  he  does  on  top  of  a  full  day's  work. Five  days  a  week Mustafa 
drives  a  parcel  run  for  Australia  Post,  out  of  the  depot  by  six  in  the  morning.  The 
coaching comes after, three evenings a week, and two more when he trains himself, 
home well after eight. 
Four years ago, Mustafa Mohmand arrived in Australia having forgotten football. Now 
he spends  most evenings teaching it. And most nights, when he  sits down to  study 
footage of his own team and the sides they are about to face, a small person sits down 
beside him. Hoora his daughter, now four, watches the players move across the screen 
and asks him why that one ran that way and not the other, how they scored, how many 
are allowed on the field. 
He already knows what he wants for her. "One day, one of my team players will be 
my daughter," he says. 
 
 
Between 2021 and 2024, COMPACT connected hundreds of young people from 
migrant and refugee backgrounds through more than 40 activities across 
Newcastle and Hunter Region. 
 